==================================================
Lab 3: Blind OS Command Injection with Time Delays
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A website has a feedback form. When you submit it, the server runs a shell command using the details you entered.
The command's output is not shown in the response, so we cannot read anything back directly.
Instead, we use time to confirm the injection: if our command makes the server wait, the injection worked.

Goal: make the server respond with a 10 second delay.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When you submit feedback, the browser sends the name, email, subject, and message to the server.
The server likely uses the email value inside a shell command, for example to log or process the submission.
The normal response is the same page either way, so there is no visible feedback from the command.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The email field is placed directly into a shell command without any filtering.
Shells support several ways to chain commands, so text we add to the email value can run as a separate command.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Double pipe (||): In a shell, this runs the second command only if the first one fails.
The first part, takethis@123, is not a valid command, so it fails, and the second command runs.
Ping: The ping command sends network packets to an address. With -c 10, it sends 10 packets,
one per second, so it takes roughly 9 to 10 seconds to finish. This gives us a measurable delay.
CSRF token: The form includes a hidden token that the server checks. The script must fetch it
from the feedback page first and send it back with the submission.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

We put this value into the email field:

takethis@123||ping -c 10 127.0.0.1||

The server builds a command that roughly looks like:

mail takethis@123||ping -c 10 127.0.0.1||

The first part fails, so the shell runs ping. Ping takes around 10 seconds to finish, and the server
does not respond until it does. That delay is the proof that our command ran.
The trailing || is there so the shell stays valid after our command.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and go to the feedback page.
Fill in any name, subject, and message, then enter the payload above in the email field.
Submit the form. The response takes about 10 seconds to arrive, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It creates a session and asks for the lab's base URL.
* It loads the feedback page with a GET request, then searches the HTML for the CSRF token using a regular expression.
* It stops if no token is found, since the submission would be rejected.
* It builds the payload with the token and the injected email value.
* It sends a POST request to the submit endpoint with a 20 second timeout, which is longer than the delay.
* It prints the response text. Because the output is blind, this text is not the important part.

=============================
ONE THING TO KEEP IN MIND
=============================

The script does not measure the delay itself, so it cannot tell you whether the injection worked.
You can check the time the server took using response.elapsed, which holds the time between sending
the request and receiving the response. Printing this value shows whether the delay happened.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not pass user input into shell commands. Use the language's built-in functions for the task instead.
If a command must be used, pass user input as a separate, escaped argument, never as part of the command string.

Other good practices:

Validate input, allowing only the characters an email address can contain.
Run the application with the lowest permissions it needs.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Blind injection can be confirmed by timing, even when no output is shown.
The double pipe runs the next command when the first one fails.
Never trust input from a user, even when the output is hidden from them.
